Q6. The shared commit rule

Why this is dangerous: git reset --hard rewrites commit history and deletes commits, which causes severe merge conflicts, broken histories, and lost work for teammates who have already pulled those commits.

What should they use instead: git revert <commit-hash> (which safely creates a new commit that reverses the changes without altering existing history).

The rule to remember: Never use git reset (especially --hard) to rewrite history on commits that have already been pushed to a shared/public repository.

Q7. Build an undo workflow

Reset to undo the commit: git reset --soft HEAD~1 (or git reset HEAD~1 to undo the commit and unstage at the same time)

Remove the file from staging: git reset HEAD <filename> (or git restore --staged <filename>)

What to do before committing again: Remove the sensitive information (like the API key) from the file—or add the file to .gitignore so it is never tracked—and verify with git status and git diff before making a clean commit.
